  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis highlight an upper-\nlevel wave tracking across the Northern Plains as high pressure \naloft continues to the build across the Desert Southeast. At the \nsurface, a stationary front spans from southeast Kansas into north \ncentral Oklahoma. With this boundary expected to slowly shift \nnorthward, we continue to see the potential for a handful of \nthunderstorms developing later this afternoon into the evening \nhours across far southern Kansas given increasing convergence \namid weakly capped conditions. While these should remain \nisolated in nature and primarily along/south of US-54, plenty of\ninstability and ample shear may promote strong to severe \nactivity capable of producing large hail and damaging winds. \nHowever, it remains unclear how much destabilization will be \nallowed to take place as ongoing elevated showers and storms \nobserved across portions of south central Kansas continue early \nthis afternoon. If these are able to persist into the evening, \nchances for activity associated with the surface boundary should\ndiminish.\n\nGuidance over the past several days has been fairly consistent with \nhighlighting a mid-level baroclinic zone setting up over the area \nlater tonight. Showers and storms are forecast to develop \nalong/ahead of this feature as the LLJ strengthens, and with solid \nelevated thermal and wind profiles at/above 700 mb, strong to severe \nstorms will be possible particularly east of Interstate 135 and \nsouth of Interstate 70. The strongest activity will likely be \ninitially capable of producing large hail, though as storms cluster \nthe main threat will shift toward heavy rainfall producing localized \nflooding concerns.\n\nStorms will gradually shift southeast throughout the night into \nFriday morning with some lingering activity possible across far \nsoutheast Kansas perhaps even into the early afternoon per the \nHREF/REFS. Otherwise, dry and hot conditions are forecast to return \nto the area beginning Saturday as the upper ridge builds eastward. \nResultant increasing thicknesses will promote high temperatures \nranging from the middle 90s to low 100s throughout the area through \nat least Tuesday. Long range guidance is beginning to come into \nagreement in a sagging front arriving Tuesday night into Wednesday \nas a mid-level shortwave races over the Great Lakes. This may bring \nrain chances back into the area and knock temperatures back into the \nlow to mid 90s by midweek. Stay tuned as additional information \nbecomes available.",
